1. Get data from sources

Power BI can connect to sources such as:

Excel
CSV
SQL Server
MySQL
Web/API
SharePoint
Cloud databases

Example:

Excel + SQL Server + CSV → Power BI

2. Profile the data

Before transforming it, you examine the data to understand its quality.

You check:

Data types
Null/missing values
Errors
Duplicates
Distribution of values
Number of rows/columns
3. Clean the data

Using Power Query, you can:

Remove duplicates
Replace values
Remove errors
Handle null values
Remove unnecessary columns
Rename columns
Change data types
Split columns
Merge columns

For example:

Raw Data

Name       Age    City
Vinay      23     Bangalore
Rahul      null   Bangalore
Vinay      23     Bangalore

After cleaning:

Name       Age    City
Vinay      23     Bangalore
Rahul      24     Bangalore
4. Transform the data

You may need to reshape the data before loading it into the model.

Common transformations:

Merge queries → combine columns from related tables
Append queries → stack rows from similar tables
Pivot / Unpivot
Group By
Filter rows
Add calculated/custom columns
5. Load the prepared data

After transformation:

Data Source → Power Query → Clean & Transform → Semantic Model

Then you can create:

Relationships
Measures
DAX calculations
Reports and visualizations
